===persian===

بر دارها...
خطّی و آونگی...
و نسیمی که بوی لاشه‌ها را...
تا... دهن‌درّهٔ کفتارها می‌بُرد...
و باد می‌آمد...
و... باد می‌آمد...
آنقدر تند که...
کفتارها مرد مصلوب را...
با موهای بلندش نشناختند...
آنگاه باد...
صلیبِ تنها را از روی تپه بُرد...
۱۳۴۷

===machine===

On the gallows...
linear, and pendulous...
and a breeze that was carrying the smell of the carcasses...
to... the yawning mouths of the hyenas...
And the wind was coming...
and... the wind was coming...
so hard that...
the hyenas did not know the crucified man...
by his long hair...
And then the wind...
carried the lone cross off the hill...
1347

===lantern===

On the gallows...
linear, and pendulous...
and a breeze that was carrying the smell of the carcasses
to... the yawning mouths of the hyenas...
And the wind came...
and... the wind came...
so hard that
the hyenas did not know the crucified man
by his long hair...
And then the wind
carried the lone cross off the hill...

1347

===translation===

===footnotes===

خطّی و آونگی — *linear, and pendulous.* Before the poem will say what is hanging there, it says what
a painter sees: a line, and a weight swinging from it. That refusal to name the bodies for two lines
is the whole method of the poem, and the reason the horror arrives when it does.

دهن‌درّه is a *yawn* — literally a tearing-open of the mouth. The breeze carries the smell of the
carcasses not to the hyenas' jaws but to their yawning, which is worse: they are bored. And when the
wind rises hard enough, what they fail to do is *recognise* the crucified man by his long hair —
مصلوب and صلیب put a crucifixion in a book that otherwise has none, so the long hair invites the
reading it invites. The wind finishes the job the hyenas could not, and carries the empty cross off
the hill.
